--- !u!114 &1626851584
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1626851582}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 0
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: '<b>The Cape Floral Region is under siege.</b>


    Invasive wasps - <i>Vespula
    germanica</i> and <i>Polistes dominula</i> - are establishing themselves across
    the Western Cape. They out-compete native pollinators for the same food and nesting
    sites, and the damage does not stop with the species they displace. Remove a
    pollinator and you touch everything that depended on it.


    <b>The real problem
    is that people cannot tell them apart.</b>


    Native and invasive wasps look
    alike to an untrained eye. So pest control gets applied indiscriminately, and
    the beneficial native species - the ones doing the pollinating - are destroyed
    alongside the invaders. Good intentions, wrong target.


    <b>What you do here.</b>


    You
    manage a native colony: claim territory across a map of the Cape, gather resources,
    train wasps into roles, and defend against invasive hives expanding toward you.
    Before you can act on a species you have to identify it, and identification is
    earned - the more encounters you survive, the more of its biology the codex releases
    to you.


    Misidentify a native as invasive and biodiversity takes the hit.
    That cost is the argument: the mechanics are not decoration on a lesson, they
    <i>are</i> the lesson.


    <b>Why a game.</b>


    Competitive exclusion and
    trophic cascades are non-linear. A diagram shows you the end state; it cannot
    make you feel a slow collapse you caused three decisions ago. Playing it out
    is what makes the dynamic legible.


    <size=85%><i>Built at Open Window for
    PP410, drawing on entomological data from SANBI and the University of Stellenbosch.</i></size>'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 0b8f754176fe04541a7a44fcb76911f2, type: 2}
